We define the minimal identification error for a distribution $\cD$ and language collection $\cC$ as follows:
\begin{align}
    &\iderrmin(\cA, \cD, \cC, n) := \p_{\rS \sim \cD^n\hspace{-0.2em},\hspace{0.1em} \rr}\big[ L_{\cA(\rS, \rr)}\not\in \optarg_{\cD,\cC} \text{ or } \exists L\in  \optarg_{\cD,\cC} \text{ s.t. } L\subsetneq L_{\cA(\rS, \rr)}\big]. \label{eqn:minimal-agnostic-identification-benchmark}
\end{align}

Now consider the following sub-class of distributions in $\cP_{Opt}$, which satisfy the added condition that at least one optimal language have a tell-tale in the support:
\begin{align}\label{eq:minimal-distributions}
    \cP(\cC) = \{\cD \in \cP_{Opt}(\cC) \mid \exists L \in \optarg_{\cD,\cC}, \exists T_L\in \cT_L, T_L\subseteq \supp(\cD)\}.
\end{align}

